An operand-taking keyword ending its line (`:RETURN`, `:IF`, `:WHILE`)
takes its operand from the next line: no `;` is forced after the keyword
and the operand line takes one continuation level. A bare `:RETURN`
followed by a keyword really has no operand and is terminated
(fmt.semicolon_enforcement and fmt.indent_style, issue #255).
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE Pick;
:PARAMETERS nValue;
:DECLARE nResult;
nResult := 0;
:WHILE
nResult < nValue;
nResult += 1;
:ENDWHILE;
:IF
nResult > 10;
:RETURN
nResult;
:ENDIF;
:RETURN
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE Pick;
	:PARAMETERS nValue;
	:DECLARE nResult;
	nResult := 0;
	:WHILE
		nResult < nValue;
		nResult += 1;
	:ENDWHILE;

	:IF
		nResult > 10;
		:RETURN
			nResult;
	:ENDIF;
	:RETURN;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
15:1-15:9 error unexpected_token: Unexpected keyword ':ENDPROC' - expected an expression
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
15:1-15:9 error unexpected_token: Unexpected keyword ':ENDPROC' - expected an expression
